\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{cyan}{\textcolor {cyan}{o}}\ "Events before droplet splashing on a solid surface" says: The major simplifications for a typical millimetre-sized droplet with impact velocity of order $1ms^{-1}$ are (i) slender geometry for the gas film, (ii) the neglect of viscous forces in the liquid, (iii) the neglect of surface tension forces at the liquid–gas interface and (iv) the neglect of nonlinear inertia in the liquid. With respect to viscous forces, the liquid Reynolds number is of order $\text  {Re} = \rho R V / \mu \sim 1000$, where $\mu ,\rho $ are the liquid viscosity and density respectively; hence inertial forces in the fluid initially overwhelm viscous forces, and we therefore neglect viscosity. Similarly, $\text  {We} = \rho V^2/\sigma \sim 100$ shows surface tension is negligible}{3}{section*.3}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid3}{19562753}{42703429}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{pink}{\textcolor {pink}{o}}\ Hicks and Purvis further assume that we don't care about surface tension and gravity, and that we are incompressible. The former of these assumptions only really holds as the interface is smooth and flat, but this breaks down under high curvatures (interface wants to pull itself back together! HP doesn't go into this but we might!) The middle of these holds because something something Freud Number. The latter of these holds (CITE Mandre et al 2009) that this holds when $P_a \sqrt  [3]{\frac  {\mu _g}{\rho _lUR}} \gg 1$.}{3}{section*.4}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid5}{19562753}{25315231}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{green}{\textcolor {green}{o}}\ This seams really easy to surpass! - Thoroddsen et al (2005) and Dam and Le Clerc (2004) shows the impact velocity only needs to be more than $1ms^{-1}$! - This is okay though, as droplets in cloud formations have really slow collision velocities. If we really want to do this though, we modify the continuity equation in the lubrication equation, to consider a barotropic argument.}{3}{section*.5}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid6}{19562753}{20201476}
