\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{pink}{\textcolor {pink}{o}}\ Okay, here's what's going on: the liquid is inviscid, so we lose slip. Hence, as we are dealing with a fluid-fluid interface, NEITHER fluid can have no-slip conditions. We only enforce impermeability via the lack of boundary conditions available from Laplace's equation. However, the moving interface creates the kinematic boundary equation (forces a streamline) and pressure matching boundary condition. This is made simpler as $h(r,t)$ is not a one-to-many function?}{6}{section*.18}\protected@file@percent }
